  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The models are validated against a microscopic simulation rather than empirical, real-world traffic data from active pickup and drop-off facilities.",
      "what": "This study develops stochastic models, including renewal-reward processes and continuous-time Markov chains, to calculate the throughput capacities of passenger pickup and drop-off facilities while accounting for delays from conflicting vehicle movements. It also introduces a continuous-time Markov decision process to optimize operational policies and validates these analytical models using a microscopic trajectory-based discrete-event simulation.",
      "why": "It provides a mathematical framework to evaluate and compare different facility layouts and operational policies, offering a systematic way to design high-capacity alternatives to traditional curbside operations as demand from ride-hailing and autonomous vehicles grows."
    }
  },
